WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.197 --> 00:00:02.243
This is a local proof of concept.

2
00:00:02.243 --> 00:00:06.520
It compares two ways of turning a receipt image into structured data.

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:10.798
Everything you will see runs on this laptop, with synthetic receipts.

4
00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:16.663
I choose a synthetic pharmacy receipt that was deliberately saved at low

5
00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:17.374
resolution,

6
00:00:17.374 --> 00:00:19.313
and run both extraction paths.

7
00:00:20.025 --> 00:00:25.142
The direct path gives the image itself to a small local vision-language model,

8
00:00:25.142 --> 00:00:27.701
Qwen3-VL, with four billion parameters.

9
00:00:27.701 --> 00:00:31.506
The OCR-assisted path first reads the image with RapidOCR,

10
00:00:31.506 --> 00:00:34.393
then gives only that text to the same model,

11
00:00:34.393 --> 00:00:37.870
with the same instructions and the same field schema.

12
00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:42.331
So the only difference is what the model sees. Both are running now;

13
00:00:42.331 --> 00:00:43.512
the timer is live.

14
00:00:43.812 --> 00:01:05.543
[Live extraction running on this laptop. The wait is not cut.]

15
00:01:05.743 --> 00:01:11.125
Here are the two drafts, side by side. Direct is blue, OCR-assisted is orange.

16
00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:14.645
They disagree on the receipt number and on the tax.

17
00:01:14.645 --> 00:01:20.165
The OCR path returned no tax at all, so its subtotal and total no longer add up,

18
00:01:20.165 --> 00:01:21.614
and both are flagged.

19
00:01:22.322 --> 00:01:27.650
The OCR text shows why. The tax line came out as unreadable characters,

20
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:32.077
and the model did not invent a value. OCR errors propagate:

21
00:01:32.077 --> 00:01:35.003
the model cannot recover what OCR lost.

22
00:01:35.712 --> 00:01:40.627
Now the receipt number. The image shows RX-I07618.

23
00:01:40.627 --> 00:01:47.312
The direct path read the I as a one. The OCR path read the X as a Y.

24
00:01:47.312 --> 00:01:48.983
Neither is right.

25
00:01:49.993 --> 00:01:53.125
Every value is a draft until a person approves it.

26
00:01:53.125 --> 00:01:57.947
The reviewer starts from the direct values, types the correct receipt number,

27
00:01:57.947 --> 00:02:00.703
and adds a note. The edited field is marked.

28
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.442
On approval, the app keeps what each path extracted next to the approved values,

29
00:02:06.442 --> 00:02:11.169
records the human change, and exports a JSON record with an integrity hash.

30
00:02:12.806 --> 00:02:18.733
Across thirty synthetic receipts, the direct path got 170 of 180 fields right,

31
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:24.811
and the OCR path 167: too close to call. The OCR path was faster on this laptop.

32
00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:27.623
Both failed on look-alike characters,

33
00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:31.118
and six errors per path raised no flag at all.

34
00:02:31.118 --> 00:02:34.309
That is why a person makes the final call.

35
00:02:35.572 --> 00:02:39.718
The code, the experiment and the full results are in the repository.

36
00:02:39.718 --> 00:02:40.937
Thanks for watching.
